# Jetpack Compose support

## Goal

Render a Compose app's UI, not just its Activity lifecycle. Today
`setContent` is answered with a no-op, so an app finishes `onResume` and
exits having produced nothing on screen.

## Why this is the shape of the problem

A composable's body is ordinary bytecode — it already executes. What is
missing is the machinery it writes *into*:

- The compiler rewrites every `@Composable` call into emissions against a
  `Composer`: `startRestartGroup` / `endRestartGroup`, `startNode` /
  `endNode`, `rememberedValue`, `changed`, `consumeTextNodeFactory`, ...
- The `Composer` is what turns those emissions into a tree of `LayoutNode`s.
- That tree is what a `MeasurePolicy`/`LayoutNode` draws through a graphics
  backend.

So the work is a `Composer` implementation, then a render backend. Not
"implement Compose" in the abstract — a specific, nameable missing class.

## Stages

### Stage 1 — make composables actually run
`setContent` stops being a no-op. Extract the `Function2` content lambda
from the call arguments and invoke it against a real `Composer` object.
Any Composer method we have not implemented answers with an inert value so
the composable runs to completion instead of dying on a missing method.

Success: a Compose `Activity` composes its content headlessly. Measurable
by instruction count and by the set of unresolved Composer methods, which
tells us the actual surface area rather than guessing at it.

### Stage 2 — a recording Composer
Implement the group/node protocol so the emitted tree can be captured:
`startNode`/`endNode` for structure, `consume*NodeFactory` for leaves.
Dump the result as a text tree. This proves the app produced a real view
hierarchy before any graphics exist.

Success: `apknative ui app.apk` prints the composable tree with node
types and content.

### Stage 3 — measure and layout
Give nodes a size, run measure/layout passes, resolve placement and
intrinsic measurement. Still text-only output.

### Stage 4 — draw
Rasterise. Skia is the Android native renderer; on this machine there is
no Vulkan and only `llvmpipe`, so this would be software-rendered and
slow even when correct.

## Constraints to keep in mind

- No Vulkan on this host — software rendering only. Affects Stage 4.
- Release APKs are R8-obfuscated, so Composer internals appear under
  mangled names in a real APK; matching is on signature, not name.
- Every stage must keep `./vps.sh ci` green. No stage may regress the
  existing APK-launch and JVM-parity suites.

## Verification

- `./vps.sh ci` — build, JVM parity, APK launch
- `apknative verify` clean on the F-Droid DEX files
- F-Droid instruction count and missing-shim set, tracked as the
  Stage 1 signal
